const standard = (title,path,n) => layout(title,path,`<section class="hero inner">${image(n)}<div class="container hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ROOF REPAIRS / INFORMATION</p><h1>${heading(title)}.</h1><p>Clear context for Perth roof repairs, organised around observable details, materials and useful questions for an enquiry.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Make an enquiry</a></div></section><section class="section grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">SCOPE / ${String(n+1).padStart(2,'0')}</p><h2>Read the condition before choosing a path.</h2><p>Roof surfaces, edges, drainage lines and junctions can each give a different piece of information. This page is a practical reading guide, not a diagnosis. Note what is visible, where it appears and whether it changes after weather or routine use.</p><p>Ellis Services Group presents Perth roof repair context in plain language so a conversation can begin with useful detail. Materials, access and the relationship between roof and drainage all shape which questions matter next.</p></div>${image(n+1)}</section><section class="section grid texture"><div>${image(n+2)}</div><div><p class="eyebrow">OBSERVE / COMPARE</p><h2>Keep the observation specific.</h2><ul><li>Look for changes at joins, edges, penetrations and water paths.</li><li>Separate a visible surface mark from the wider roof context around it.</li><li>Record material type when known, without assuming a cause.</li><li>Use the same reference point when comparing conditions over time.</li></ul></div></section><section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">REPAIR PATHS</p><h2>Three ways to frame a repair conversation.</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Repair</h3><p>A focused discussion can begin with one observed detail and its surrounding context.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Targeted replacement</h3><p>Related components can be considered together where material continuity matters.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Broader remediation</h3><p>A wider view may help when several observations appear across a roofline.</p></article></div></section>${related}`);

let homeWords = `<section class="hero home">${image(0)}<div class="container hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">PERTH ROOF REPAIRS / FIELD NOTES</p><h1>ROOF REPAIR INFORMATION, BUILT FOR CLEARER NEXT STEPS.</h1><p>Read the roofline, drainage and material details before an enquiry begins.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Make an enquiry</a><a class="text-link" href="/roof-repairs/">Explore roof repairs</a></div></section><section class="route-rail"><div class="container"><a href="/roof-repairs/"><b>01</b> Roof repairs</a><a href="/roof-leak-repairs/"><b>02</b> Leak repairs</a><a href="/gutters-downpipes/"><b>03</b> Gutters & downpipes</a><a href="/repair-options/"><b>04</b> Repair options</a></div></section><section class="section grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE ROOF AS A SYSTEM</p><h2>Information organised by the details people can actually see.</h2><p>Roof repair conversations are clearer when they begin with the part of the building that has changed. A tile edge, a metal sheet, a ridge line, a flashing junction or a drainage run each offers a distinct starting point. Rather than treating every observation as the same kind of issue, this site groups information by material, location and water path. That makes it easier to describe what you have noticed without drawing a conclusion too early.</p><p>Ellis Services Group uses this Perth roof repairs reading room to make the first step less opaque. It does not replace an assessment, and it does not turn a photograph or a brief description into a diagnosis. It helps frame a useful enquiry: what is visible, where is it located, which material is involved, and what else is nearby?</p></div>${image(1)}</section><section class="section texture"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">SERVICES / READING PATHS</p><h2>Find the page that matches the part you are looking at.</h2><div class="cards">${[['Roof repairs','/roof-repairs/'],['Tile roof repairs','/tile-roof-repairs/'],['Metal roof repairs','/metal-roof-repairs/'],['Ridge capping & repointing','/ridge-capping-repointing/'],['Roof maintenance','/roof-maintenance/'],['Roof inspection','/roof-inspection/']].map(([t,l],i)=>`<article class="card">${image(i)}<h3>${t}</h3><p>Material and roofline context arranged for clearer questions.</p><a href="${l}">Explore roof repairs</a></article>`).join('')}</div></div></section><section class="section image-section"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">OBSERVABLE SIGNALS</p><h2>Use a signal as a prompt to look more closely.</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Water path</h3><p>Notice where water appears to travel after rain and where that path meets a roof edge or drainage component.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Surface change</h3><p>Compare a change in colour, texture or alignment with the material around it.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Junction detail</h3><p>Look at the relationship between flashing, capping, sheets, tiles and penetrations.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Drainage line</h3><p>Follow the visible route from roof edge through gutter and downpipe components.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">REPAIR-PATH MATRIX</p><h2>Different observations can lead to different discussions.</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Repair</h3><p>A focused repair discussion starts with the immediate detail, its material and the nearby roof condition. It is a useful way to describe one identifiable observation.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Targeted replacement</h3><p>Where a related component is part of the same roofline, a conversation may include how adjacent materials connect and shed water together.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Broader remediation</h3><p>When observations occur in more than one area, it can help to map them across the roofline before deciding which questions to ask next.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section gallery-strip"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">MATERIAL / DETAIL / DRAINAGE</p><h2>Illustrative roof textures and lines.</h2><div class="gallery-grid">${image(0)}${image(1)}${image(2)}${image(3)}</div><p>These visuals help show roof materials and drainage details in general terms. They are not presented as work history, customer material or proof of an outcome.</p></div></section><section class="section texture"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">READING ROOM</p><h2>Guides for a more informed conversation.</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Metal roofing in Perth</h3><p>Consider how visible material details shape the questions you ask.</p><a href="/news/metal-roofing-perth/">Read the guide</a></article><article class="card"><h3>Gutter warning signs</h3><p>A practical way to observe drainage components without jumping to a cause.</p><a href="/news/gutter-warning-signs/">Read the guide</a></article><article class="card"><h3>Roof leak inspection</h3><p>Learn how to organise notes about a visible water-related observation.</p><a href="/news/roof-leak-inspection/">Read the guide</a></article></div><a class="text-link" href="/news/">View all articles</a></div></section><section class="section grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">MATERIALS</p><h2>Material language can sharpen an enquiry.</h2><p>Tiles, metal sheets, ridge elements, flashings, gutters and downpipes behave as connected parts of a roofline. Naming a material is not a claim about its condition. It is simply a useful way to identify the location of an observation. If the material is unknown, a description of its shape, position and relationship to other parts can be just as helpful.</p><p>In Perth roof repair context, a rain event can make drainage details easier to see, while dry conditions can make surface texture and alignment easier to compare. Notes do not need to be technical. A simple record of what changed, where it is visible and what lies next to it gives a useful foundation for a direct call or email.</p></div>${image(2)}</section><section class="section"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">FAQ / STARTING POINTS</p><h2>Six common questions, with the full list in the FAQ.</h2><div class="faq-list">${['What should I note before making an enquiry?','Can a roof observation be described without knowing the material?','Why do gutters and downpipes belong in roof repair context?','What is ridge capping?','What is flashing?','Where can I find Ellis Services Group contact details?'].map(q=>`<details><summary>${q}</summary><p>Ellis Services Group provides Perth roof repair context through pages like this one. Start with what you can see, avoid assuming the cause, and use the direct contact details when you are ready to discuss it.</p></details>`).join('')}</div><a class="text-link" href="/faq/">Read the full FAQ</a></div></section>`;
