const gutterDecisionGuide = `<section class="section resource-guide-detail"><div class="narrow"><h2>How Ellis assesses gutter overflow in Perth</h2><p>Gutter overflow can call for clearing, a local repair or replacement of a damaged section. Ellis Services Group checks the gutter run, outlet and connected downpipe together, then defines the work from the obstruction or defect found. Recurring overflow after cleaning needs a closer look at the drainage route.</p><h3>Clearing leaves and outlets</h3><p>Leaves and debris at a gutter outlet can interrupt the path into the downpipe. We check where material has collected and whether the connected outlet and downpipe are clear. Where an obstruction is the issue and the components remain sound, clearing the affected route is the relevant maintenance work. Water marks or retained water also prompt a check of the gutter condition and fall; removing leaves alone does not correct a damaged joint or a low section.</p><h3>Repairing joints and local components</h3><p>Water escaping through a join, a loose connection or a damaged local section needs a component assessment. Ellis checks the joint, surrounding material, supports and downpipe connection before defining the repair. A sound adjoining run supports a focused repair; corrosion or deterioration extending beyond the joint can call for replacement of the affected section. The scope identifies that section and its connections rather than assuming the whole system needs replacing. Our <a href="/gutter-repairs/">gutter repairs</a> and <a href="/downpipe-repairs/">downpipe repairs</a> pages explain the connected services.</p><h3>When overflow returns after cleaning</h3><p>Repeated overflow is a reason to inspect the full roof-to-gutter-to-downpipe path. Ellis considers the water arriving from the roof, the gutter fall, outlet position and condition, downpipe flow and overflow arrangements. Rainfall and the roof area feeding a run matter to that assessment. A visible overflow point alone does not establish which component needs changing, so the proposed work follows the site findings. See our <a href="/gutters-downpipes/">gutters and downpipes service</a> for the wider drainage scope.</p><h2>From rain observations to a repair plan</h2><ol><li>Record the affected elevation and the rain conditions from a safe position. The <a href="/news/drainage-after-rain/">drainage after rain guide</a> helps organise those observations.</li><li>Ellis checks the gutter, joints, outlet and downpipe, including visible water marks and the condition of adjoining eaves or fascia.</li><li>We define whether the work is clearing, a local repair, replacement of a damaged component or a broader drainage correction, and explain the access and connected items involved.</li><li>The work scope includes checks relevant to the repaired route, such as clear outlets, connected downpipes and the joints addressed. Confirm any remaining drainage or building concerns in the quote.</li></ol><h2>What to send for a gutter repair quote</h2><p>Include your Perth suburb, the affected gutter run and the locations of nearby outlets and downpipes. Describe whether water escapes at a joint, over the edge or at a downpipe connection, and whether it happens in light, steady or heavy rain. Safe photos of corrosion, separated joins and water marks at the fascia, eaves or walls help us plan the inspection.</p><p>Access also affects the scope: tell us the building height, gates or restricted areas, and whether the gutter is above a patio or other roof. Ask the quote to identify the cleaning or repairs included, the sections and connections being replaced, and any adjoining damage that requires a separate item. <a href="/contact/">Request an Ellis gutter and downpipe assessment</a> with a short description. Existing ground-level photos and rain notes are optional. Keep all observations at ground level; roof and elevated gutter work needs controlled access.</p></div></section>`;
